### Targeting Code Coverage with CoversClass Attribute

Source: https://github.com/sebastianbergmann/phpunit-documentation-english/blob/13.2/src/code-coverage.md

Use the `#[CoversClass]` attribute on a test class to specify which class's code should be included in coverage reports. The `#[UsesClass]` attribute indicates other classes that are expected to be used by the covered code.

```php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\CoversClass;
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\UsesClass;
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

#[CoversClass(Invoice::class)]
#[UsesClass(Money::class)]
final class InvoiceTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testAmountInitiallyIsEmpty(): void
    {
        $this->assertEquals(new Money, (new Invoice)->amount());
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Ignoring All Code Coverage with CoversNothing Attribute

Source: https://github.com/sebastianbergmann/phpunit-documentation-english/blob/13.2/src/code-coverage.md

Apply the `#[CoversNothing]` attribute to a test class when its tests should not contribute to code coverage. This is useful for integration tests where you want coverage to be generated by smaller, more focused tests.

```php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\CoversNothing;
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

#[CoversNothing]
final class IntegrationTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testRegisteredUserCanLogIn(): void
    {
        // ...
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Skip Test if PHPUnit Version Mismatched

Source: https://github.com/sebastianbergmann/phpunit-documentation-english/blob/13.2/src/attributes.md

The RequiresPhpunit attribute skips tests when the PHPUnit version does not meet the specified requirement. This ensures compatibility with specific PHPUnit features or changes.

```php
#[RequiresPhpunit('>= 10.1.0')]
```

```php
#[RequiresPhpunit('^10.1')]
```

### Writing Tests for PHPUnit > Skipping tests > Skipping tests using attributes

Source: https://github.com/sebastianbergmann/phpunit-documentation-english/blob/13.2/src/writing-tests-for-phpunit.md

Beyond programmatic skipping, PHPUnit supports attributes to define preconditions for test execution. These attributes, located in the PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes namespace, allow developers to skip tests based on PHP version, required extensions, configuration settings, operating system, or the presence of specific methods and functions.

